===Etymology===
From "[[bourbon whiskey]]", originally "[[Bourbon whiskey]]", of disputed provenance. Generally taken to derive from [[w:Bourbon County, Kentucky|Bourbon County]], [[Kentucky]], but possibly also from [[w:Bourbon Street|Bourbon Street]] in [[New Orleans]], [[Louisiana]].<ref>Kiniry, Laura. "[http://blogs.smithsonianmag.com/food/2013/06/the-boozy-history-of-where-bourbon-really-got-its-name-and-more-tips-on-americas-native-spirit/ Where Bourbon Really Got Its Name and More Tips on America’s Native Spirit]". ''Smithsonian.com''. 13 June 2013. Accessed 12 September 2013.</ref>

Both derive from the [[French]] [[w:Bourbon dynasty|Bourbon dynasty]], named for the lordship of {{etyl|fr|en}} ''Bourbon l'Archambault''. The town's name derives from {{etyl|cel-gau|en}} [[w:Borvo|Borvo]], a local Celtic deity associated with hot springs, from {{etyl|cel-pro|en}} {{m|cel-pro|*borvo||froth, foam}}.

